  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13254v1</id>
    <title>Dependable Long-Horizon LLM Agents: A Trajectory-Centered Empirical Study of Where Errors Enter and Which Controls Reach Them</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T21:25:08Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13254v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13254v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>When an LLM agent fails part-way through a long task, the usual analysis records a symptom: a failure-mode label, or the agent and step judged responsible. It does not say where the error entered, which state carried it to the symptom, whether anything in the trajectory contradicted it, or which control could have caught it. We define an episode record that ties a candidate fault to its origin, propagation, detection, response and consequence, located by operational phase and state domain, and that names the control families whose observation and reach cover it. We turned the record into a written annotation protocol and applied it to 95 failed runs of six multi-agent systems from the public Who&amp;When and MAD corpora, reading each trajectory in full. Faults enter early (median at 0.18 of the run), mostly as wrong decisions or fabrications over task state, and in 37 of 88 Who&amp;When runs before the step the corpus' human labels mark as decisive. In 72% of failed runs the trajectory already contained evidence against the error before the failure; in 88% of those no agent acted on it. Something flagged a deviation in 31% of runs, but no response removed the deviated state. Independent judges reading the unused evidence, task contracts fixed at admission, and re-checking beliefs against the environment reach most episodes; restoring the agent's own state would have sufficed in 35 of 95 runs. A text scan of the raw corpus also shows that 45% of the hand-crafted Who&amp;When failures ended with a provider content-filter crash after which the harness emitted a final answer no agent produced, a failure class that neither symptom taxonomies nor responsible-agent labels represent. Protocol, records and scripts are included.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T21:25:08Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Shaohuang Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jinghao Wen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xulei Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xun Jiao</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14113v1</id>
    <title>Looking One Step Ahead: An Anticipation-guided Agent for Object State Readout in Videos</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T14:05:39Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14113v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14113v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Understanding the progress of an activity requires tracking how the objects involved change over time. Object state readout provides this information by describing a target object's states and transitions across video frames. Although multimodal large language models (MLLMs) generate detailed visual descriptions, these descriptions may leave the attributes needed to distinguish successive states insufficiently specified. In this paper, we propose look-ahead state readout (LASER), an anticipation-guided agent for object state readout that uses possible next-step changes to determine which attributes to inspect in the present. Given temporally ordered frames and a target object, LASER converts anticipated changes into targeted visual questions and inspects the current frame to establish attribute values. It then checks state readouts and forecasts against independent observations of subsequent frames, refining a state record used to generate the final description. Reusable textual observation skills guide this process and are refined by tracing discrepancies with reference captions to the responsible instructions. Candidate revisions are selected on separate videos based on improved caption quality without increased contradictions with reference facts. Model parameters remain frozen throughout adaptation and inference, and the revised skills remain fixed during evaluation. Experiment results and further analysis on OSCaR and PLM RCap show consistent improvements of LASER over strong baselines and existing approaches, which demonstrates the effectiveness of our approach.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T14:05:39Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>13 pages, 2 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yuanhe Tian</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yan Song</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13236v1</id>
    <title>NCP: Verifier-Guided Optimization of Weight-Space Control Policies</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T21:05:21Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13236v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13236v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present the Neural Control Plane (NCP), a closed-loop system that observes language-model execution, synthesizes candidate weight-space intervention rules, evaluates them, and maintains a versioned table of admitted policies. NCP separates a pretrained model's inference data plane from the control policy that selects temporary parameter interventions. W-ISA supplies transactional execution and parameter restoration; NCP supplies telemetry, candidate synthesis, admission criteria, priorities, and rule lifetime management. The optimization state consists of active rules and an archive of evaluated candidates, while base-model parameters are restored after each request. In a local SmolLM-135M-Instruct experiment, NCP generated three candidates from development telemetry and evaluated them on separate task examples and a 4,095-target WikiText-2 slice. One projection reduced validation token repetition from 0.4133 to 0.2191, with a perplexity change of -0.0574%. All candidates produced zero newly correct exact answers, so the configured admission gate archived all three and retained the original active policy. Fresh-process repetition and saved-table resumption reproduced the measured outcomes. NCP provides an executable framework for studying recursive self-improvement at the control-policy level through explicit synthesis, verification, and state transitions.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T21:05:21Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Ekrem Aksoy</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12918v1</id>
    <title>IntentTwin: Deconfounding Candidate Support and the Oracle-to-Deployment Gap in Offline Web Behavior Prediction</title>
    <updated>2026-10-27T18:39:23Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12918v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12918v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Offline web-behavior benchmarks frequently insert the demonstrated action into a finite candidate set and then measure reranking. We show that this protocol can create a candidate-support confound: when the target is outside training support while all distractors are sampled from that support, support membership identifies the gold action without modeling behavior. We formalize this effect with support-identifiability rate (SIR), construct support-balanced candidate sets, and evaluate a target-free dual-path proposer that combines support retrieval with open-vocabulary DOM/LM proposals. On filtered OPeRA (4,282/469/992 train/dev/test steps), the original protocol has SIR 1.0000 on target-OOV rows and a support-only rule reaches 1.0000 OOV Exact@1. Support balancing reduces SIR to 0.0000; the strongest conditional reranker obtains 0.7214 overall Exact@1, 0.7988 on IV rows, and 0.5668 on OOV rows. The target-free dual-path system reaches Proposal Recall@16 0.6915 and end-to-end Exact@1 0.5212, compared with oracle conditional Exact@1 0.7450. On Mind2Web, the same audit yields SIR 0.2380 and an oracle-to-proposal gap of 0.2125. A 200-row semantic audit labels 0.7150 of exact-key OOV targets as semantically novel, 0.2450 as locator/string variation, and 0.0400 as ambiguous (Cohen's $κ=0.8173$). These measurements show why support composition must be controlled alongside candidate accuracy when offline results are used to reason about deployment.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-27T18:39:23Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>26 pages, 10 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Miaobo Hu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shuhao Hu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiaobo Guo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xin Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Bokun Wang</name>
    </author>
