  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13615v1</id>
    <title>Synapse-Control: Making Agent Experience Actionable with Lightweight Runtime Control</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T05:50:12Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13615v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13615v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language model agents reuse execution experience to improve reliability on complex tool-based tasks. Systems such as Sentry call large models to generate failure diagnoses before retrieving advice, adding inference cost to experience reuse. We present Synapse-Control, an agent runtime whose lightweight control plane selects advice and determines when to send it to the task-executing model. Rules shortlist relevant advice, and a lightweight 1.7B classifier fine-tuned on teacher-labeled state-suggestion pairs scores those candidates instead of generating a diagnosis. Micro-contracts encode reusable interface checks, such as required arguments or valid options, without prescribing complete task programs. A Prompt Value Table records earlier guidance and feedback to limit repeated suggestions through cooldown and token-budget checks. Across 1,308 attempts per method in AppWorld and WebShop, Synapse-Control achieves 270 successes (29.8% more than Base), replaces 4,987 generative diagnosis calls, and reduces auxiliary control time relative to Sentry by 50.2% and 51.0%, respectively.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T05:50:12Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>21 pages, 8 figures, 18 tables, including appendices</arxiv:comment>
